# Review repairs: distinguish source transport, thermal charge susceptibility and statistical resolvent.
REVIEW_OVERRIDES={
524:dict(
 title='Normalized source moments with boundary mass exchange',
 claim_key='normalized_source_moments_with_boundary_mass_exchange',
 math='On fixed Omega, delta rho=-div(rho xi)+sigma; m2=I2/M, I2=int_Omega r^2rho dV. delta m2=[2int rho r dot xi dV-int_boundary(r^2-m2)rho xi dot n dA+int_Omega(r^2-m2)sigma dV]/M.',
 inputs='Smooth density on a fixed bounded domain, outward unit normal n, finite M>0, displacement xi, and prescribed infinitesimal mass injection sigma. Permit boundary flux; the closed-source case sets xi dot n=0 and sigma=0. Choose one compact shell fixture with a nonzero boundary contribution.',
 target='Derive the normalized second-moment response from the continuity variation, including delta M=-int_boundary rho xi dot n dA+int sigma dV. Isolate transport, boundary exchange and injection terms; recover the closed-source formula only after imposing its conditions.',
 negative='Allow a nonzero boundary mass flux but retain the fixed-M moment formula.',
 first_principles='Derive the source-size response from local mass conservation on a fixed physical domain. Boundary flux and injection are independent specified data, not a fitted gravitational source correction.',
 closure_bridge='A changing finite-aperture baryonic source must enter the same filtered field equation with its actual mass and size variations; this identity prevents normalization drift from masquerading as gravity response.',
 continuation=['If promising, propagate the boundary-exchange moment into the low-k filtered source expansion.', 'If promising, compare fixed-aperture and material-aperture force predictions under the same physical source current.', 'If failed, specify a conserved source current and domain motion before using a mass-normalized observable.']),
861:dict(
 title='Regulator dependence of thermal carrier charge susceptibility',
 claim_key='regulator_dependence_of_thermal_carrier_charge_susceptibility',
 math='For normalized classical charged normal-mode actions I_k^+,I_k^-: H=sum omega_k(I_k^++I_k^-), Q=sum(I_k^+-I_k^-), Z=integral exp[-beta(H-mu Q)]dGamma. chi_Q(K)=partial_mu<Q>|_0=beta Var(Q)|_0=2T sum_|k|<=K omega_k^-2 in this normalization, beta=1/T.',
 inputs='Existing carrier fields only, a fixed finite periodic three-dimensional volume, stable quadratic branch with omega_k>0 and ultraviolet omega_k~c_s|k|. Set c=k_B=1 explicitly. Count physical charged branches from the action; do not add particle species. Keep K finite before any limit, and |mu|<min omega_k. Use the canonical mode measure derived from the quadratic symplectic form.',
 target='Derive the charge susceptibility from the actual diagonal U(1) charge, including mode degeneracies and normalization. Determine its leading K dependence and whether fixing mean charge permits a nonzero regulator-independent small-mu response at fixed temperature. Do not repeat the total-energy equipartition calculation in AS1423.',
 negative='Infer a finite continuum charge susceptibility merely because the thermal mean charge vanishes at mu=0.',
 first_principles='Derive the Gibbs charge response from the existing action and its canonical U(1) generator. Temperature, chemical-potential ensemble, finite volume and regulator remain declared assumptions; thermal accessibility is not inferred.',
 closure_bridge='A statistical carrier closure using charge compressibility must match a finite, physically normalized susceptibility to the common action; total-energy regularization alone does not establish that response.',
 continuation=['If promising, derive the charge-response matching condition under a physical coarse-graining change.', 'If promising, determine the charge fluctuation correction to a long-wavelength auxiliary response using the same finite regulator.', 'If failed, replace equilibrium closure by an explicitly finite-energy occupation law and derive its charge response before claiming continuum thermodynamics.']),
866:dict(
 title='Finite Lomax Markov resolvent spectral susceptibility',
 claim_key='finite_lomax_markov_resolvent_spectral_susceptibility',
 math='On 0<=u<=U, f0=Z_U^-1(1+u)^-3 and L=D0 f0^-1 partial_u(f0 partial_u), reflecting boundaries. Let -L psi_n=lambda_n psi_n in L2(f0). For centered bounded A and energy drive -h(t)A, chi_A(omega)=beta sum_n>=1 lambda_n |<A,psi_n>_f0|^2/(lambda_n-i omega), with h(t)=Re[h0 exp(-i omega t)].',
 inputs='Finite U>0,D0>0,beta>0; choose A(u)=u-<u>_f0. The perturbation must modify the reversible drift so the constant-drive stationary density is proportional to f0 exp(beta h A); its rate is a specified stochastic model, not derived carrier dynamics. Use two finite spectral resolutions only as a check of the analytic representation.',
 target='Derive the resolvent spectral representation from the perturbed generator and reflecting boundary current, then prove the exact static spectral-weight sum chi_A(0)=beta Var_f0(A). Give the positive-frequency absorptive sign and identify how each eigenvalue enters without substituting an arbitrary rational susceptibility.',
 negative='Insert a phenomenological Debye relaxation rate while claiming it was derived from the specified Lomax generator.',
 first_principles='Derive the susceptibility from one explicitly reversible finite-domain stochastic generator and its physical drive convention. The stochastic mobility D0, beta and any bridge to gravitational carrier time remain independent inputs.',
 closure_bridge='This supplies a normalized statistical response function that can be compared with, but not identified with, the independently derived same-action baryon/carrier response of AS1071.',
 continuation=['If promising, quantify the spectral-weight error of a finite-mode susceptibility approximation on a declared frequency interval.', 'If promising, determine whether the U->infinity limit preserves the static sum and the finite-frequency response separately.', 'If failed, repair the driven boundary current or generator coupling before comparing a stationary CDF with physical response.'])}
for r in records:
    override=REVIEW_OVERRIDES.get(int(r['id'][2:]))
    if override:
        o=override.copy();target=o.pop('target');negative=o.pop('negative')
        r.update(o);r['steps'][1]=target
        r['steps'][3]='Test the altered premise: '+negative+' Exhibit its violated equation or omitted term.'
        r['checks'][1]='Negative control: '+negative
        r['completion']='Deliver the explicitly displayed response identity and its domain, or an exact obstruction from the stated equations; preserve the independent empirical and ensemble inputs.'
        r['kind']='derivation'

